WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.449
A tragedy has struck as Hector discovers his
friend has overdosed.

00:00:03.449 --> 00:00:04.890
How can she be saved?

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.380
Well first Hector must know what opioids are.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.210
Say someone falls to the ground and hurts
her knee.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:12.660
In this case the body will release what are
called endogenous opioids to help her out

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:13.670
against the pain.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:15.760
These include endorphins, enkephalins and
dynorphin.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.330
However, an opioid is anything that binds
to an opioid receptor.

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:22.560
Therefore there are opioids which come from
outside our body but interact with the opioid

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:23.610
receptors in the same way.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.450
These are called exogenous opioids.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.060
There are three opioid receptors to which
they bind: mu, delta and kappa receptors;

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:33.089
and they are all a part of a group of receptors
called G-protein coupled receptors or GPCR.

00:00:33.089 --> 00:00:35.460
Let’s view a model of this.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:39.050
This part is the receptor itself, with 7 helices
which go through the membrane.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:42.649
On the outside of the cell, there is a cavity
to which an agonist, like an opioid, binds.

00:00:42.649 --> 00:00:46.920
On the intracellular side, the receptor is
coupled to a G-protein, which has an alpha,

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.179
beta and gamma subunit.

00:00:48.179 --> 00:00:50.559
When an agonist binds, the G protein activates.

00:00:50.559 --> 00:00:53.980
The GDP molecule bound to the alpha subunit
is released and replaced with GTP.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:57.949
The protein dissociates into the alpha subunit
and the beta-gamma subunit, both of which

00:00:57.949 --> 00:00:59.920
can now interact with other protein pathways.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.559
For example, alpha interacts with adenylyl
cyclase, and the alpha protein can stimulate

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:06.110
or inhibit, based on the type.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:11.110
This enzyme uses ATP to produce cAMP. cAMP
is the secondary messenger, which is involved

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:12.590
in many different responses.

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:16.840
An opioid can use this mechanism to inhibit
this enzyme and then prevent pain signals.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:21.000
Eventually, the body becomes dependent on
and tolerant of the drugs, leading the body

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.050
to want more.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:25.420
Addictions often begin with prescription drugs,
and then go out of control.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.130
This has led to an opioid crisis.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:31.350
To prevent misuse, people must only take the
prescribed amount of drugs, not share medications

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.310
and stay educated on the opioid crisis.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:38.370
It is important to solve this crisis because
as many as 128 people die each day from an

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:39.640
overdose in the U.S.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.220
But how can Hector’s friend be saved from
an overdose?

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:46.120
An opioid is an agonist, but there are also
antagonists such as naloxone which can bind

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.520
to an opioid receptor in the same way, but
not affect the G protein.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.020
Why is this important?

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.430
Well, now that it is bound to the receptor,
the opioid can no longer bind, preventing

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:55.430
its effects

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.579
Knowing this, it is possible for Hector to
save his friend’s life.

00:01:58.579 --> 00:02:00.819
He just needs to be prepared and stay informed.

